# Files that get COPY'd into the Linux forecast container must check out with
# LF endings, on every box, whatever the local `core.autocrlf` says.
#
# ABL-596: this checkout has `core.autocrlf=true`, so the LF blobs in the repo
# land in the working tree as CRLF. `docker build` copies the working tree, so
# the image gets `#!/bin/bash\r`, the kernel looks for an interpreter literally
# named `/bin/bash\r`, and every container off that image dies immediately:
#
#     $ docker run --rm docker-forecast:latest
#     exec /entrypoint.sh: no such file or directory     (exit 255)
#
# With `restart: unless-stopped` in docker-compose.yml that is a crash loop,
# not a running scheduler -- no forecasts, from an image that built cleanly.
# Pin the endings rather than relying on whoever builds having the right git
# config. Deliberately narrow: only the files that cross into the container.
*.sh        text eol=lf
docker/crontab text eol=lf

# ABL-715: the second class of file whose exact bytes are load-bearing.
#
# Provenance records name the bytes they graded by SHA-256 -- ABL-438's
# `results_sha256`, ABL-443's `input_record.sha256`, ABL-444's `record_sha256`
# -- and the tests re-hash the file to check it. Those hashes were recorded on
# this workstation, where `core.autocrlf=true` had turned the LF blobs into
# CRLF in the working tree, so they were hashes of bytes that exist on exactly
# one box. Measured: every recorded digest matched the CRLF working tree and
# none matched the LF blob, so the records could not verify on a Linux checkout
# -- CI, another workstation, or inside the serving container.
#
# A record that pins bytes nobody else can reproduce is not doing the job it was
# written for, so the pin goes on the file instead of the reader: with `eol=lf`
# the checked-out bytes are the stored bytes everywhere, and one digest is
# correct on every box. Repo-wide for `*.json` rather than per-directory,
# because the class is "a file some record may hash", not a location, and no
# JSON here is ever read by something that wants CRLF.
#
# Renormalising cost nothing: no `*.json` blob contained CRLF, so this changes
# what checkout writes, never what git stores. (`*.py` is deliberately NOT here
# -- 12 tracked source blobs, `src/forecaster.py` among them, DO hold CRLF, so
# the same line would rewrite serving source. That is its own change.)
*.json      text eol=lf
